In this lesson, we'll look at why trig functions are so predictable: it's all down to their symmetry and periodicity. First up, periodicity. Both sine and cosine are repeating waves that complete a full cycle every two pi radians, which is the same as three hundred and sixty degrees. So once you know what they do over one cycle, you know them forever. Next, even and odd symmetry. Cosine is what's called an even function, which means it's a mirror image across the vertical axis. If you flip the input to its negative, you get the same value out. Sine, on the other hand, is odd, meaning it has symmetry about the origin, so flipping the input flips the output as well. Then we have the supplementary and complementary identities. These are little shortcuts that let you take an angle outside the first quadrant, like something in the second or third quadrant, and rewrite it in terms of an angle between zero and ninety degrees. Finally, remember the unit circle is your friend. Every angle gives you a point whose coordinates are cosine and sine, and the symmetries of the circle are exactly where all these identities come from. Now let's see it in action.